<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Fustat</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Fustat was the first Arab capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amr-ibn-al-as">Amr ibn al-As</a> around 641 during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rashidun-caliphate">Rashidun Caliphate</a>'s conquest. It became a major commercial city, and it remained an important centre after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fatimid-caliphate">Fatimid Caliphate</a> founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a> nearby. It was later absorbed into Cairo.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/fustat.jpg" alt="Fustat" width="315" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Rappoport, A.S. (Angelo Solomon), 1871-1950 VIAF (71482297) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Fostat-329.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Fustat lay on the eastern bank of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a>, just north of the Roman fortress of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a>, beside what is now Old Cairo. According to legend a dove laid an egg in the tent of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amr-ibn-al-as">Amr ibn al-As</a> just before he marched on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a> in 641. He took it as a sign, left the tent untouched, and on his return had his soldiers pitch their tents around it. The name Misr al-Fustat is popularly translated as city of the tents, though this is not an exact translation. The Mosque of Amr was later built on the site of his tent, and was the first mosque in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>'s capital had been <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a> for nearly a thousand years. After Alexandria fell in September 641, Amr founded the new city. Its early population was almost entirely soldiers and their families, and it was laid out like a garrison, serving as a base for conquering <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a> until Qayrawan was founded in 670. Fustat grew as a series of tribal areas, khittas, around the mosque, with most settlers coming from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yemen">Yemen</a>. Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasids</a> the seat of government moved to al-Askar, and later, under Tulunid rule, to al-Qatta'i, but Fustat regained its place in 905.</p><p>Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fatimid-caliphate">Fatimids</a> it prospered, and the geographer Ibn Hawkal wrote in 987 that it was about a third the size of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a>. It had shaded streets, gardens and markets, and tall residential buildings, some reported at seven storeys. From 975 to 1075 it was a major centre of Islamic art and ceramics. In 1168, with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusader-states">Crusader</a> king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amalric-i-of-jerusalem">Amalric I of Jerusalem</a> approaching, the vizier Shawar ordered the city burned. The historian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-maqrizi">al-Maqrizi</a> wrote that the blaze lasted 54 days. The philosopher <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maimonides">Maimonides</a> settled in Fustat in 1166 and wrote his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mishneh-torah">Mishneh Torah</a> there in 1180.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>After the fire Fustat declined, and under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluks</a> it was used as a rubbish dump. Its remains were absorbed into the growing city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a>, and it is now part of the district of Old Cairo. Few buildings from its time survive, and the Mosque of Amr has been so extensively rebuilt that nothing of the original remains. Excavations have found trade goods from as far away as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam">Vietnam</a>, and finds are shown in Cairo's Museum of Islamic Art.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Fustat” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Cairo was founded north of <b>Fustat</b> as a palatine capital, and the al-Azhar mosque was begun in 970.</blockquote><a href="/empire/fatimid-caliphate">Fatimid Caliphate · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>This system kept conquered agriculture productive and channelled its surplus into stipends that sustained the Arab military establishment in purpose-built garrison cities, Kufa and Basra in Iraq and <b>Fustat</b> in Egypt, which were at once military camps, administrative centres and growing commercial hubs.</blockquote><a href="/empire/rashidun-caliphate">Rashidun Caliphate · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Some glass and carnelian beads have been traced to the workshops of <b>Fustat</b> in Old Cairo.</blockquote><a href="/culture/igbo-ukwu-culture">Igbo-Ukwu culture · Contact &amp; Exchange</a></li><li><blockquote>The invasion took Alexandria but failed before <b>Fustat</b>, and the remnants returned to Ifriqiya in May 915.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/january-24">24 January in history</a></li><li><blockquote>The Fatimid general Jawhar al-Siqilli led the Friday prayer in <b>Fustat</b> in the name of Caliph al-Mu'izz li-Din Allah, symbolically completing the conquest of Egypt.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/july-9">9 July in history</a></li><li><blockquote>The Ikhshidids governed from <b>Fustat</b> and held southern Syria and the Hejaz, including Mecca and Medina, contesting northern Syria with the Hamdanids.</blockquote><a href="/state/ikhshidid-emirate">Ikhshidid emirate</a></li></ul></section>
